    "['vrrp vrid [parameter1] interface [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/3', 'parent_command': ['redundancy'], 'root': 'redundancy', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"The equivalent Cisco command for the Huawei VRRP interface binding configuration would be:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/3\\n vrrp 10 ip 10.0.0.3\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco doesn't require explicitly binding the VRRP group to an interface like Huawei does - it's implied by being configured under the interface\\n2. The virtual IP (10.0.0.3) needs to be specified in the Cisco command, which was defined separately in the Huawei configuration\\n3. Other VRRP parameters like priority, preempt, tracking would need separate Cisco commands if those features were required\", 'trans_template': 'interface GigabitEthernet1/0/3'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'vrrp 10 ip 10.0.0.3', 'parent_command': ['redundancy'], 'root': 'redundancy', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"The equivalent Cisco command for the Huawei VRRP interface binding configuration would be:\\n\\n##\\ninterface GigabitEthernet1/0/3\\n vrrp 10 ip 10.0.0.3\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Cisco doesn't require explicitly binding the VRRP group to an interface like Huawei does - it's implied by being configured under the interface\\n2. The virtual IP (10.0.0.3) needs to be specified in the Cisco command, which was defined separately in the Huawei configuration\\n3. Other VRRP parameters like priority, preempt, tracking would need separate Cisco commands if those features were required\", 'trans_template': 'vrrp 10 ip 10.0.0.3'}]]": 1,
    "['service-instance-group [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service-group 21', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'service-group 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"For Cisco devices, the closest equivalent to Huawei's `service-instance-group` would typically involve a service group or resource allocation context, though exact parity may vary. The translation would likely align with Cisco's NAT or service chaining constructs.\\n\\n##\\nservice-group 21\\nservice-policy 21\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco doesn't have a direct 1:1 equivalent for Huawei's `service-instance-group`, which binds service instances to locations. The above represents a conceptual mapping, but actual implementation may require adjusting based on the specific Cisco platform (e.g., ASR or ISR) and software version. For DS-Lite, Cisco might use `nat64` or `service insert` mechanisms instead.\", 'trans_template': 'service-group 21'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service-policy 21', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'service-policy 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"For Cisco devices, the closest equivalent to Huawei's `service-instance-group` would typically involve a service group or resource allocation context, though exact parity may vary. The translation would likely align with Cisco's NAT or service chaining constructs.\\n\\n##\\nservice-group 21\\nservice-policy 21\\n##\\n\\nNote: Cisco doesn't have a direct 1:1 equivalent for Huawei's `service-instance-group`, which binds service instances to locations. The above represents a conceptual mapping, but actual implementation may require adjusting based on the specific Cisco platform (e.g., ASR or ISR) and software version. For DS-Lite, Cisco might use `nat64` or `service insert` mechanisms instead.\", 'trans_template': 'service-policy 21'}]]": 1,
