    "['ds-lite instance [parameter1] id [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the translated Cisco equivalent command for the Huawei `ds-lite instance` configuration:\\n\\n##\\ncgn instance dslite1 id 21\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. In Cisco, DS-Lite configurations typically use `cgn instance` instead of Huawei's `ds-lite instance`\\n2. The syntax and parameters remain similar (instance name and ID)\\n3. The rest of the DS-Lite configuration would need additional Cisco-specific commands to fully replicate the Huawei functionality, but this is the direct equivalent of the requested command\", 'trans_template': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21'}]]": 1,
    "['port-range [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'nat port block size 4096', 'parent_command': ['cgn instance dslite1 id 21'], 'root': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'For the HUAWEI `port-range 4096` command in the DS-Lite context, the equivalent Cisco command would be `nat port block size 4096` under the NAT configuration mode.  \\n\\n##  \\nnat port block size 4096  \\n##  \\n\\nNote: This assumes the Cisco device is running IOS-XE or a similar version that supports DS-Lite/NAT64 with port block allocation. The exact command may vary slightly depending on the Cisco platform and software version.', 'trans_template': 'nat port block size 4096'}]]": 1,
    "['service-instance-group [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'service-group 21', 'parent_command': ['cgn instance dslite1 id 21'], 'root': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nservice-group 21\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nIn Cisco devices, the equivalent concept to Huawei\\'s \"service-instance-group\" is typically implemented using \"service-group\" commands for similar service grouping purposes. The syntax is simpler in Cisco, as it doesn\\'t require explicit service location binding in the group definition (those associations are typically handled separately in Cisco configurations).\\n\\nNote that the exact Cisco implementation might vary depending on the specific Cisco platform and features being used (like ASR or CSR series for CGNAT functionality), but \"service-group\" is the closest direct equivalent.', 'trans_template': 'service-group 21'}]]": 1,
    "['ds-lite address-group [parameter1] group-id [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 nat ds-lite pool group1 1.1.1.1/24', 'parent_command': ['cgn instance dslite1 id 21'], 'root': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"##\\nipv6 nat ds-lite pool group1 1.1.1.1/24\\n##  \\n\\nExplanation:  \\n- The Huawei `ds-lite address-group` command defines an IPv4 address pool for DS-Lite (IPv6 to IPv4 translation).  \\n- The Cisco equivalent is `ipv6 nat ds-lite pool`, where:  \\n  * `group1` is the pool name (preserved)  \\n  * `1.1.1.1/24` is the translated IPv4 address range (from Huawei's `section 0 1.1.1.1 mask 24`)  \\n- The `group-id 1` parameter isn't needed in Cisco's syntax as it's internally managed.  \\n\\nNote: Cisco requires separate commands for ALG, filtering, and other DS-Lite parameters shown in the Huawei config.\", 'trans_template': 'ipv6 nat ds-lite pool group1 1.1.1.1/24'}]]": 1,
    "['section [parameter1] [parameter2] mask [parameter3]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ip nat pool group1 1.1.1.1 1.1.1.255 prefix-length 24', 'parent_command': ['cgn instance dslite1 id 21'], 'root': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nip nat pool group1 1.1.1.1 1.1.1.255 prefix-length 24\\n##  \\n\\nThe Huawei `section 0 1.1.1.1 mask 24` command defines a NAT address pool range (1.1.1.1/24) for DS-Lite translation. The Cisco equivalent uses `ip nat pool` with the pool name, start/end IPs (1.1.1.1 to 1.1.1.255), and prefix length.  \\n\\nKey differences:  \\n1. Huawei uses `section` with index (0), while Cisco explicitly defines the range  \\n2. Both achieve the same result: allocating IPv4 addresses from 1.1.1.0/24 for NAT purposes', 'trans_template': 'ip nat pool group1 1.1.1.1 1.1.1.255 prefix-length 24'}]]": 1,
    "['ds-lite outbound [parameter1] address-group [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'ipv6 nat v6v4 source list DS-Lite pool group1', 'parent_command': ['cgn instance dslite1 id 21'], 'root': 'cgn instance dslite1 id 21', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nipv6 nat v6v4 source list DS-Lite pool group1\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\nThe Huawei command `ds-lite outbound any address-group group1` configures DS-Lite (Dual-Stack Lite) to use address group \"group1\" for IPv6 to IPv4 translation for all traffic. In Cisco, the equivalent would be:\\n\\n1. `ipv6 nat v6v4 source` - Specifies IPv6 to IPv4 translation\\n2. `list DS-Lite` - Would reference an ACL defining which traffic to translate (equivalent to \"any\" in Huawei)\\n3. `pool group1` - References the NAT pool of IPv4 addresses\\n\\nNote: In a complete Cisco implementation, you would need additional configuration for:\\n- Defining the DS-Lite ACL (access-list)\\n- Configuring the NAT pool (ip nat pool)\\n- IPv6 NAT prefix configuration\\nBut the direct equivalent of the specific command is shown above.', 'trans_template': 'ipv6 nat v6v4 source list DS-Lite pool group1'}]]": 1,
